in military press, snatch, and clean &amp; jerk determined placement."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/55185"} "Robert Fein"] " took a big lead in the military press with a lift of 105.0 kg, putting him 7.5 kg clear of the field. He maintained that lead after the snatch, but Egyptian " [:a {:href "/athletes/55545"} "Anwar Mousbah"] " had the best snatch, with 105.0 kg, and moved into second. When Mousbah also led the clean &amp; jerk with 145.0 kg, he and Fein tied with world record totals of 342.5 kg, breaking the mark of 337.5 held by Mousbah. As there was no tie-breaking method in place in 1936, Fein and Mousbah both received gold medals."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Mousbah never competed internationally again, and labored in anonymity as Egyptian radio failed to announce that he had won a medal. He was forced to carry around his gold medals for years to prove to people that he won it. Fein won a silver medal in this class at the 1937 World Championships, trailing " [:a {:href "/athletes/57026"} "Tony Terlazzo"] ", who won the " [:a {:href "/results/28828"} "featherweight division in Berlin"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Kilograms"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/55545"} "Anwar Mousbah"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EGY"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/EGY.png"}] "EGY"]] [:td {} "342.5"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/55185"} "Robert Fein"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazo